## Appendix A The radius of curvature behavior in arbitrary electromagnetic field structure

In an electromagnetic field, an asymptotic trajectory is always locally defined by the so-called Aristotelian electrodynamics (Gruzinov, 2012; Kelner et al., 2015; K18)

{\\bf v_{A}}=\\frac{{\\bf E}\\times{\\bf B}\\pm(B_{0}{\\bf B}+E_{0}{\\bf E})}{B^{2}+E _{0}^{2}}c (A1) where E_{0}B_{0}={\\bf E}\\cdot{\\bf B},\\;\\;E_{0}^{2}-B_{0}^{2}=E^{2}-B^{2}.

The particle velocity {\\bf v} continuously approaches {\\bf v_{A}} (i.e., the generalized pitch-angle \\theta decreases). The particle energy loss-rate is determined by the local R_{\\rm C}=\\gamma_{\\rm L}m_{\\rm e}c^{2}/(q_{\\rm e}B_{\\rm eff}), where \\gamma_{\\rm L},\\;m_{\\rm e},\\;q_{\\rm e} are the Lorentz factor, the mass, and the charge of the particle, respectively, c the speed-of-light, and B_{\\rm eff} reads (C16)

B_{\\rm eff}=\\sqrt{({\\bf E}+{\\bf v}\\times{\\bf B}/c)^{2}-({\\bf v}\\cdot{\\bf E}/c )^{2}}\\;. (A2)

Figure 1a shows that R_{\\rm C} depends on E,B, the angles \\psi, \\theta, and the relative orientation of {\\bf v} on the \\theta-cone (i.e., \\phi_{\\rm v}). On the one hand, the lowest R_{\\rm C}-value, r_{\\rm min}, which is achieved for high \\theta is mainly determined by the order of magnitude of the highest field value (B_{\\rm eff}=\\max(E,B)) while the variation of \\psi and \\phi_{\\rm v} produces a modulation around a mean value (Fig.1b). On the other hand, for {\\bf v}={\\bf v_{A}}, B_{\\rm eff}=0. Assuming that R_{0} is the R_{\\rm C}-value corresponding to the asymptotic flow, a small velocity component perpendicular to {\\bf v_{A}} (i.e., small \\theta) is developed that imposes R_{\\rm C}=R_{0}. For motion near the LC, the fields are \\sim B_{\\rm LC} and therefore r_{\\rm min}\\sim r_{\\rm g}.

## Appendix B Derivation of the theoretical fundamental plane relations

The spin-down power for a dipole field reads

\\dot{\\cal E}\\propto B_{\\star}^{2}P^{-4}\\;. (B1)

Assuming(i) emission at the LC near the ECS (i.e., fields of the order of B_{\\rm LC}) and taking into account that

B_{\\rm LC}\\propto B_{\\star}R_{\\rm LC}^{-3}\\propto B_{\\star}P^{-3} (B2) and R_{\\rm LC}\\propto P, we get

R_{\\rm C}\\propto\\begin{cases}R_{\\rm LC}\\propto P&\\text{CR-regime}\\\\ r_{\\rm g}\\propto\\gamma_{\\rm L}P^{3}B_{\\star}^{-1}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases} (B3) and then from Eq.(2) and (B3), we get

\\gamma_{\\rm L}\\propto\\begin{cases}\\epsilon_{\\rm cut}^{1/3}P^{1/3}&\\text{CR- regime}\\\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm cut}^{1/3}P^{3/2}B_{\\star}^{-1/2}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases} (B4)

(ii) a balance between acceleration and radiative losses

E_{\\rm BLC}B_{\\rm LC}\\propto\\gamma_{\\rm L}^{4}R_{\\rm C}^{-2} (B5) where E_{\\rm BLC} is the E_{\\rm acc} in B_{\\rm LC} units. From Eqs.(B2)-(B5), we get

E_{\\rm BLC}\\propto\\begin{cases}\\epsilon_{\\rm cut}^{4/3}P^{7/3}B_{\\star}^{-1}& \\text{CR-regime}\\\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm cut}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases} (B6) and consequently the luminosity of one-particle reads

L_{\\gamma 1}\\propto E_{\\rm BLC}B_{\\rm LC}\\propto\\begin{cases}\\epsilon_{\\rm cut}^{ 4/3}P^{-2/3}&\\text{CR-regime}\\\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm cut}B_{\\star}P^{-3}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases} (B7)

(iii) that the total \\gamma-ray luminosity L_{\\gamma} scales with the number of emitting particles in the dissipative region, N_{d}=n_{\\rm GJ\\text{-}LC}V_{d}, where n_{\\rm GJ\\text{-}LC} is the Goldreich-Julian number-density at the LC, n_{\\rm GJ\\text{-}LC}\\propto n_{\\rm GJ\\star}R_{\\rm LC}^{-3}\\propto B_{\\star}P^{ -1}R_{\\rm LC}^{-3} where n_{\\rm GJ\\star} is the Goldreich-Julian number-density on the stellar surface and V_{d} the volume of the dissipative region, which we assume that V_{d}\\propto R_{\\rm LC}^{3}. Thus, N_{d}\\propto n_{\\rm GJ\\star}\\propto B_{\\star}P^{-1} and taking into account Eq.(B1), we get

L_{\\gamma}\\propto L_{\\gamma 1}B_{\\star}P^{-1}\\propto\\begin{cases}\\epsilon_{ \\rm cut}^{4/3}B_{\\star}^{1/6}\\dot{\\mathcal{E}}^{5/12}&\\text{CR-regime}\\\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm cut}\\;\\dot{\\mathcal{E}}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases} (B8)

We note that according to Eqs.(B1) and (B8), L_{\\gamma} may be a function of any 2-combinations of the (\\dot{\\mathcal{E}},\\;B_{\\star},\\;P) variable-set. Moreover, taking into account that \\dot{\\mathcal{E}}\\propto P^{-3}\\dot{P}, L_{\\gamma} may also be expressed as a function of the directly observable quantities

L_{\\gamma}\\propto\\begin{cases}\\epsilon_{\\rm cut}^{4/3}P^{-7/6}\\,\\dot{P}^{1/2}&\\text {CR-regime}\\\\ \\epsilon_{\\rm cut}\\,P^{-3}\\dot{P}&\\text{SR-regime}\\end{cases}

Nonetheless, any of these relations are equivalent.